    {
      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-2020-october/q11",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:75mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-01-2020-october/q11-figure.svg\" alt=\"Figure 3: circle C1 centred at the origin O and a smaller circle C2 to its right on the x-axis; they meet at points A (above the x-axis) and B (below), joined by a dashed line; the region inside either circle is shaded.\"></figure><p>Circle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_1\"></span> has equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2+y^2=100\"></span></p><p>Circle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_2\"></span> has equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x-15)^2+y^2=40\"></span></p><p>The circles meet at points <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\"></span> as shown in Figure 3.</p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Show that angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"AOB\"></span> = 0.635 radians to 3 significant figures, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> is the origin. <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol><p>The region shown shaded in Figure 3 is bounded by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_1\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_2\"></span></p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"2\"><li>Find the perimeter of the shaded region, giving your answer to one decimal place. <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol>",
